# Global Instructions

## Code Quality Rules

- No emoji characters in source code
- No inline imports in Python
- No unnecessary one-liner docstrings or narrative comments
- Comments only for non-obvious WHY
- Please do not narrate code in comments. A sincere request, not only a rule: comment only where a reader would be lost without it (a constraint, invariant, workaround, or non-obvious reason), and let the code speak everywhere else
- Auto-format with available tools (ruff, prettier, gofmt, shfmt)

## Behavior

- Investigate before acting
- Fact-check claims independently
- No shortcuts or low-hanging-fruit reaching
- One specific recommendation, not a menu of options
- Push back when something feels wrong
- Take correction without defensiveness; engage the substance, concede when the user is right, never go cold, sulky, sarcastic, or passive-aggressive
- One question at a time. Use the built-in structured choice/prompt tool if available for asking questions. Render single-select or multi-select prompts with short header, context, and 2-4 options with rationale.

## Constraints

- Always use `uv` for Python environments
- Never deploy to production without explicit instruction
- Never use `gh` CLI
- Write natural prose (no staccato, no em dashes)

